migrations are managed with golang-migrate

Command to create migrations:

migrate create -ext sql -dir sql/migrations -seq create_users_table

Use -seq (sequential) naming. It is a required flag: the create default writes
timestamped names (format 20060102150405), which CI rejects as a mixed scheme
and which permanently break -seq afterward (the next sequence number becomes
the 14-digit timestamp). CI enforces that all versions share one digit length
(scripts/check-migrations.sh).

golang-migrate only applies migrations with a version newer than the highest
one already recorded in a database's schema_migrations table. A migration with
an older version that is merged after a newer one has deployed is silently
skipped. A version number is therefore consumed the moment its migration is
merged to the release branch, and merged migrations are immutable: CI refuses
edits, renames, and deletions of existing migrations and requires every new
version to be strictly greater than everything already on the base branch.

If your branch's migration fell behind one that merged first, rename its .up
and .down files to the next free -seq number before merging — no database
action is needed since it has not been applied anywhere yet. If your branch
predates a merged migration, rebase so the branch carries it. CI enforces all
of this (scripts/check-migrations.sh --base).

Command to generate db models:
sqlc generate